package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** First new expansion serial. Original, teen-safe mystery in ten complete chapters. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch36 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_city_of_unfinished_maps",
            title = "The City of Unfinished Maps",
            tagline = "A missing street, a retired mapmaker, and a neighbourhood determined to remember its own story.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Street That Wasn't There", listOf(
                    "Mina noticed the mistake while helping her class prepare a history exhibition. The old map showed a narrow street between the market and the river, but every modern map left the space blank. The street had a name, Lantern Lane, and a row of tiny squares that looked like houses. Mina walked the route after school. There was no lane, only a wall covered with climbing vines and a faded blue sign. The sign had been painted over so many times that its letters were almost invisible.",
                    "Her classmate Kelechi thought it was a printing error. Mina disagreed because the same lane appeared on three maps made in different decades. At the library, she found a newspaper photograph showing children carrying lanterns along the very street. The caption called it the neighbourhood's reading lane. Mina copied the date and source into her notebook. She did not yet know why the street had disappeared, but she knew the answer would involve more than a careless cartographer.",
                    "The librarian, Mrs Danjuma, studied the photograph for a long moment. She said the map collection had been donated by a retired surveyor named Mr Adeyemi, who lived near the old bus depot. Before Mina left, Mrs Danjuma warned her that old records could be incomplete and memories could disagree. Mina wrote that down too. Her investigation would need patience, evidence, and people willing to tell the parts of the story they remembered."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Mapmaker's Workshop", listOf(
                    "Mr Adeyemi's workshop smelled of paper, wood polish, and rain. Maps covered the walls, each one marked with careful notes in blue pencil. When Mina showed him the photograph, he recognised Lantern Lane immediately. He had surveyed the district as a young apprentice and remembered measuring its small courtyards. He also remembered that the lane had been removed from the official map after a drainage project, although he could not explain why.",
                    "He opened a flat drawer and found a tracing sheet with the lane drawn in delicate lines. Several doorways had been marked with circles, and beside each circle was a letter. Mr Adeyemi explained that the letters identified community notice points, places where residents shared news and borrowed books. The lane had been more than a shortcut. It had been a little public space maintained by the people who lived there.",
                    "Mina asked why the mapmaker had kept the tracing. Mr Adeyemi said he had promised a neighbour that the lane would not be forgotten. He had never found a suitable way to return the map to the community. Mina offered to bring her class back, but he insisted they first learn what happened to the residents. A map could show where people lived; it could not explain why they had left."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "Names in the Margin", listOf(
                    "The tracing sheet contained six surnames. Mina and Kelechi searched the public library directory and found two families still living nearby. An elderly tailor, Mrs Bello, remembered Lantern Lane as a place where neighbours watched one another's children and shared tools. She said the drainage work had flooded several homes, and the council had offered temporary relocation while repairs were made.",
                    "Mrs Bello remembered returning to find the lane fenced off. Some residents had been moved permanently, while others had accepted flats in a new estate. She did not know who had made the final decision. Her account was valuable, but Mina recorded it as an interview rather than treating it as proof of every detail. Kelechi asked whether anyone had kept letters from the relocation period.",
                    "Mrs Bello brought out a biscuit tin filled with postcards and folded notices. One notice announced a residents' meeting that had been cancelled. Another listed a new meeting place, but the date had been crossed out. The papers suggested that residents had tried to organise a response. Mina photographed the documents with permission and promised to return copies. The mystery was becoming less about a vanished road and more about a community whose voices had been lost in official paperwork."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Meeting That Moved", listOf(
                    "The crossed-out meeting date led Mina to the district archive. A clerk helped her locate the council's public minutes from that year. The first folder contained a short entry saying the meeting had been moved because the hall was unavailable. The next folder recorded that the replacement meeting had taken place, but the attendance sheet was missing. A later note described the relocation as agreed by residents.",
                    "Mina noticed that the dates did not line up. The notice in Mrs Bello's tin announced a meeting two weeks after the date in the minutes. The clerk explained that draft minutes were sometimes filed separately from approved records. They searched the catalogue and found a box of correspondence from the same month. Inside was a letter from a community organiser asking for another meeting because many families had not received the first notice.",
                    "The letter did not prove that the relocation had been forced, but it showed that the official summary was incomplete. Mina asked the clerk how corrections could be added to an archive. He explained that a documented community submission could be attached to the record. Mina and Kelechi now had a practical goal: gather reliable accounts and make sure the archive preserved the disagreement instead of hiding it."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Photograph Album", listOf(
                    "A retired teacher named Mr Eze invited the students to see an album he had kept since the year of the drainage works. The photographs showed Lantern Lane on an ordinary afternoon: a book table, flower pots, a hand-painted sign, and neighbours repairing a bench. One picture showed a public notice pinned to a post. Its date matched the letter in the archive.",
                    "Mr Eze remembered that the lane's residents had asked for the drainage project to be redesigned so that the homes could remain. Engineers had explained that the old channel was unsafe and that the work needed a clear route. Some families supported relocation because their houses had suffered repeated flooding. Others wanted repairs without losing their community. The album showed both a meeting and a farewell gathering, but no photograph could tell Mina what each person had chosen.",
                    "The students labelled every photograph with the information Mr Eze could confirm and marked uncertain details clearly. He was pleased that they did not turn the album into a simple story of heroes and villains. The past, he said, was usually more complicated than a school poster. Mina began to understand that an honest map needed space for several experiences, even when they did not agree."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Line Beneath the Road", listOf(
                    "Mr Adeyemi returned to the workshop tracing and noticed a second pencil line beneath Lantern Lane. He had forgotten that he had drawn the old drainage channel before the project began. The line curved behind the wall and met a culvert beside the market. A modern maintenance plan showed the same culvert, which meant the original lane could be located precisely even though the buildings had gone.",
                    "The students asked a geography teacher to help compare the old and new plans. She explained the difference between a historical route and a current public right of way. They should not encourage anyone to enter the fenced area or treat an old drawing as permission to cross private property. Instead, they could mark the former lane from the public pavement and ask the council whether a small information plaque was possible.",
                    "The teacher helped them prepare a transparent overlay showing the lane, the drainage channel, and the modern street layout. The overlay made one detail obvious: the new road had been widened, but a narrow strip of public land remained beside the wall. It was enough space for a sign, a bench, and a safe place to display the history. For the first time, Mina could imagine a future for the memory of Lantern Lane."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Voices We Hadn't Heard", listOf(
                    "Before presenting their findings, Mina asked Mrs Bello whether the class had heard from everyone who had lived on the lane. Mrs Bello suggested contacting the residents' association at the estate where several families had moved. The association secretary arranged a small listening session. Some residents remembered the relocation as a welcome chance to live in safer homes. Others remembered losing familiar neighbours and the place where their children had learned to read.",
                    "One man, Mr Yusuf, brought a copy of the original compensation form. It showed that each household had received a separate offer and that some forms were still unsigned. The document helped explain why memories differed. There had not been one single experience. The students asked permission before recording names and agreed to use only the details each person wanted shared publicly.",
                    "Mina changed the exhibition plan. Instead of writing that Lantern Lane had simply been erased, the display would explain the flood risk, the engineering work, the relocation choices, and the community's efforts to be heard. Kelechi added a timeline that distinguished confirmed dates from remembered events. Their teacher said the revision was stronger because it respected people rather than forcing their lives into a neat conclusion."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "A Place on the New Map", listOf(
                    "The students presented their overlay and research to the neighbourhood council. They did not ask for the old houses to be rebuilt or suggest that the drainage project should be undone. They proposed a small public marker at the remaining strip of land, with a short account of Lantern Lane and a QR code linking to the oral histories and scanned documents.",
                    "The council asked practical questions about maintenance, permission, and whether the display might obstruct the pavement. Mina's team had measured the available space and included a simple design that kept the walking route clear. Mr Adeyemi volunteered to check the historical map, while the residents' association offered to review the wording. The council agreed to consider the proposal after a safety inspection.",
                    "There was one disagreement about the title. Some residents wanted the marker to say 'The Lost Lane'; others preferred 'Lantern Lane Remembered'. Mina suggested asking the people who had lived there. At the next listening session, the second title received more support because it described what remained without pretending the past could be restored exactly. The group adopted it and began preparing the final text."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Missing Page", listOf(
                    "While checking the dates for the marker, the archive clerk discovered a page filed inside the wrong council folder. It was the missing attendance sheet from the relocation meeting. The page listed residents who had attended and included a note that a second consultation was required because several households were absent. That note had never appeared in the approved summary.",
                    "The discovery did not reverse the project or settle every disagreement, but it confirmed that the process had been more complicated than the short official entry suggested. The clerk added the page to the correct file and accepted the students' community submission as a companion record. Mrs Bello and Mr Yusuf reviewed the summary together. They disagreed about some details, but both agreed that the new account was fairer.",
                    "Mina added a final panel to the exhibition explaining how records can become incomplete and how careful researchers correct them. She credited the archive staff, the former residents, the mapmaker, and everyone who had checked a date or shared a photograph. The students had not discovered a secret villain. They had uncovered a missing part of the public record and helped return it to the people it concerned."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "Lanterns on the Wall", listOf(
                    "On the day the marker was unveiled, the residents brought small paper lanterns and placed them along the pavement. The display showed the old lane as a fine blue line, with the modern road in grey. Beside it were short quotations approved by former residents, including one about the reading table and another about the relief of moving away from repeated floods.",
                    "Mr Adeyemi stood beside Mina as the council representative read the new wording. It said Lantern Lane had been a real neighbourhood, that its residents had experienced the relocation in different ways, and that their records and memories deserved to remain part of the district's history. The QR code opened the archive collection, where visitors could explore the maps, photographs, minutes, and interviews.",
                    "Later, Mina returned to the wall alone. The lane was still absent from the modern street map, but it was no longer missing from the story of the city. She understood that maps were not only drawings of roads. They were choices about what people noticed, preserved, and passed on. Her class had not put Lantern Lane back where it once stood. They had made sure the next generation would know it had been there."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
